# D120 Batch 2: STI (15) + Geriatrics (20) + Men's Health (15) + Cancer Screening (15) + DV (10) + Substance Use (6) = 81 questions
questions = [
  # ===== STI SCREENING AND TREATMENT (15) =====
  {"topic":"STI","course":"D120","q":"A 22-year-old sexually active woman has a routine STI screen. Her gonorrhea/chlamydia NAAT returns positive for Chlamydia trachomatis. She is not pregnant. What is the first-line treatment?","a":["Penicillin VK x 7 days","Doxycycline 100mg BID x 7 days (preferred) OR azithromycin 1g single dose (alternative — higher resistance rates)","Azithromycin 1g single dose — equally preferred as doxycycline","Ciprofloxacin single dose"],"correct":0,"rationale":"Chlamydia treatment (2021 CDC STI Treatment Guidelines): Doxycycline 100mg BID x 7 days is now PREFERRED over azithromycin single dose due to superior efficacy (especially for rectal chlamydia). Azithromycin 1g x 1 is alternative. Sexual partners should be treated. **Board tip:** Test of cure is NOT needed for uncomplicated chlamydia unless pregnant, symptoms persist, or reinfection suspected. Retest at 3 months (high reinfection rate). Expedited Partner Therapy (EPT) is legal in most states — provide Rx for partner without them being seen."},
  {"topic":"STI","course":"D120","q":"A 25-year-old has cervical discharge, pelvic pain, and a positive gonorrhea NAAT. She has no drug allergies. What is the recommended treatment per 2021 CDC guidelines?","a":["Oral azithromycin 1g single dose","Ceftriaxone 500mg IM single dose (or 1g if weight ≥150 kg) PLUS doxycycline 100mg BID x 7 days (if chlamydia not excluded)","Oral ciprofloxacin 500mg single dose","Amoxicillin 500mg x 7 days"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Gonorrhea treatment (2021 CDC): Ceftriaxone 500mg IM single dose (increased from 250mg due to rising ceftriaxone MICs). If chlamydia co-infection not excluded, add doxycycline 100mg BID x 7 days. Ceftriaxone 1g if weight ≥150 kg. **Board tip:** Dual therapy for gonorrhea (cephalosporin + doxycycline) was standard to cover chlamydia — NOW, if NAAT confirms gonorrhea only and chlamydia excluded, ceftriaxone alone is recommended. Fluoroquinolone resistance to gonorrhea is widespread — fluoroquinolones are NO LONGER recommended for gonorrhea."},
  {"topic":"STI","course":"D120","q":"USPSTF recommends chlamydia and gonorrhea screening for which population?","a":["All adults over 50 annually","Sexually active women ≤24 years (annually) and older women with risk factors (new/multiple partners); also screen MSM and others with risk factors","Men are never screened for chlamydia","Screen only if symptomatic"],"correct":1,"rationale":"USPSTF Grade B recommendation: Screen for chlamydia and gonorrhea in sexually active women ≤24 years AND in older women who are at increased risk. MSM: screen at all sites of exposure (urethra, rectum, pharynx) at least annually (more frequently if high risk). **Board tip:** Many STIs are asymptomatic — systematic screening is essential. Chlamydia is the most commonly reported notifiable STI in the U.S. PID (pelvic inflammatory disease) from untreated chlamydia causes infertility, ectopic pregnancy, and chronic pelvic pain."},
  {"topic":"STI","course":"D120","q":"A patient has a painless indurated ulcer (chancre) on the labia that appeared 3 weeks ago, with non-tender inguinal lymphadenopathy. RPR returns as reactive. What is the diagnosis and treatment?","a":["Chancroid — azithromycin","Primary syphilis (Treponema pallidum) — Benzathine penicillin G 2.4 million units IM single dose","Genital herpes — valacyclovir","Lymphogranuloma venereum (LGV) — doxycycline 3 weeks"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Primary syphilis: painless indurated chancre at infection site, non-tender lymphadenopathy. Treatment: Benzathine penicillin G 2.4 million units IM single dose. Confirm with RPR/VDRL (nontreponemal) + FTA-ABS or TP-PA (treponemal test). **Board tip:** Syphilis stages: Primary (chancre, painless), Secondary (rash on palms/soles, condyloma lata, mucous patches, flu-like), Latent (asymptomatic), Tertiary (gummas, cardiovascular, neurosyphilis). Penicillin is the ONLY acceptable treatment for all stages. Pregnant women with penicillin allergy: desensitize and treat with penicillin."},
  {"topic":"STI","course":"D120","q":"A 28-year-old woman has multiple painful vesicular lesions on the vulva. She has fever and tender inguinal lymphadenopathy. HSV-2 PCR is positive. This is her FIRST outbreak. What is the management?","a":["Topical acyclovir cream only","Oral acyclovir 400mg TID x 7-10 days OR valacyclovir 1g BID x 7-10 days for primary genital herpes — reduces severity and duration; suppressive therapy should be discussed","IV acyclovir immediately (hospitalization not required)","No treatment — HSV is a lifelong virus so treatment is futile"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Primary genital HSV: more severe first episode (bilateral lesions, systemic symptoms, longer duration). Treatment: oral antiviral (acyclovir or valacyclovir) x 7-10 days reduces duration and severity. After primary episode, discuss daily SUPPRESSIVE therapy to reduce recurrences (by 70-80%) AND transmission to partners (reduces transmission by 48%). **Board tip:** HSV disclosure: counsel patient to tell partners. Condoms reduce (not eliminate) transmission risk. Most HSV transmission occurs during asymptomatic viral shedding. Type 1 and 2 can cause genital herpes."},
  {"topic":"STI","course":"D120","q":"Which HPV strains are responsible for 70% of cervical cancers, and which strains cause genital warts?","a":["HPV 6 and 11 cause cervical cancer; HPV 16 and 18 cause warts","HPV 16 and 18 cause 70% of cervical cancers (high-risk oncogenic); HPV 6 and 11 cause genital warts (low-risk, not oncogenic)","All HPV strains cause cervical cancer equally","HPV 31 and 33 cause most cervical cancers"],"correct":1,"rationale":"HPV types: High-risk/oncogenic (types 16, 18, 31, 33, 45, 52, 58) → cervical cancer, oropharyngeal cancer, anal cancer. Types 16 + 18 = ~70% of cervical cancers. Low-risk (types 6 and 11) → genital warts (condyloma acuminata) — NOT oncogenic. **Board tip:** Gardasil 9 covers 9 strains (6, 11, 16, 18, 31, 33, 45, 52, 58) — covers ~90% of cervical cancer-causing HPV types. HPV vaccination prevents cancer — recommend to all age 9-26, shared decision-making 27-45. HPV infections are the most common STI in the U.S."},
  {"topic":"STI","course":"D120","q":"A 19-year-old male MSM presents for routine STI screening. He is HIV-negative and reports occasional condomless receptive anal intercourse. What is the appropriate HIV prevention strategy to discuss?","a":["Abstinence only","Pre-exposure prophylaxis (PrEP) — emtricitabine/tenofovir (Truvada or Descovy) reduces HIV acquisition risk by >99% when taken daily; indicated for high-risk HIV-negative individuals","Post-exposure prophylaxis (PEP) daily","Condoms alone are sufficient — PrEP not needed"],"correct":1,"rationale":"PrEP (Pre-Exposure Prophylaxis): emtricitabine/tenofovir alafenamide (Descovy) or emtricitabine/tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (Truvada) — reduces HIV acquisition risk by >90-99% when taken daily. Indicated for HIV-negative individuals with high risk (MSM with condomless sex, serodiscordant couples, people who inject drugs, etc.). **Board tip:** Before prescribing PrEP: confirm HIV-negative status, baseline renal function (eGFR), STI screen, Hep B status. Follow-up every 3 months: HIV test (detect acute infection), STI screen, BP, and renal function. Cabotegravir LA injection (every 2 months) is an alternative to daily oral PrEP."},
  {"topic":"STI","course":"D120","q":"A 32-year-old woman with gonorrhea/chlamydia cervicitis presents with bilateral lower abdominal pain, cervical motion tenderness, and adnexal tenderness. What is the diagnosis and outpatient treatment?","a":["Ovarian cyst — observe","Pelvic inflammatory disease (PID) — outpatient: ceftriaxone 500mg IM x1 PLUS doxycycline 100mg BID x 14 days PLUS metronidazole 500mg BID x 14 days","Appendicitis — surgical referral","Mittelschmerz — NSAIDs"],"correct":1,"rationale":"PID: polymicrobial ascending infection (GC, chlamydia, anaerobes, GBS). Minimum criteria: cervical motion tenderness + uterine/adnexal tenderness. Outpatient: ceftriaxone IM + doxycycline + metronidazole x 14 days. Hospitalize if: unable to take oral meds, tubo-ovarian abscess, surgical emergency, failure to improve in 72 hours. **Board tip:** PID sequelae: infertility (12% after 1 episode, 23% after 2, 40% after 3), ectopic pregnancy risk 6-10x increased, chronic pelvic pain. 'Fitz-Hugh-Curtis syndrome' = PID with perihepatitis (RUQ pain, violin string adhesions on laparoscopy)."},
  {"topic":"STI","course":"D120","q":"Syphilis screening is recommended by USPSTF for which populations?","a":["Only pregnant women","All persons at increased risk (MSM, HIV+ individuals, sex workers, persons in high-prevalence areas, those with prior syphilis) AND all pregnant women","Only those with symptoms","Adults over 60 annually"],"correct":1,"rationale":"USPSTF Grade B: Screen for syphilis in persons at increased risk, and screen all pregnant women at first prenatal visit (repeat at 28 weeks and delivery in high-prevalence areas or high-risk). U.S. syphilis rates have been rising dramatically, including congenital syphilis. **Board tip:** Syphilis serologic testing: SCREEN with nontreponemal (RPR, VDRL) → confirm with treponemal (FTA-ABS, TP-PA). OR reverse algorithm: screen with treponemal EIA → confirm with RPR. Treponemal tests remain positive for life even after treatment — RPR titer used to monitor treatment response."},
  {"topic":"STI","course":"D120","q":"A 24-year-old woman has a fishy-smelling vaginal discharge after unprotected sex. pH is 5.2, whiff test positive, and clue cells on wet mount. Treatment is prescribed. What is ALSO indicated?","a":["No partner treatment needed for BV","Screen for concurrent STIs (gonorrhea, chlamydia) since BV is associated with increased STI acquisition risk; test and treat partners only if symptomatic","Treat all sexual partners with metronidazole regardless","HIV prophylaxis"],"correct":1,"rationale":"BV increases susceptibility to STIs (HIV, gonorrhea, chlamydia, trichomonas) — women with BV should be screened for concurrent STIs. BV is NOT considered an STI per se (male partners are NOT routinely treated), but sexual activity does influence vaginal flora. **Board tip:** BV treatment: metronidazole 500mg BID x 7 days (oral) or metronidazole 0.75% gel x 5 days (vaginal) or clindamycin 2% cream x 7 days. Recurrence is common (50% at 6 months). Avoid alcohol during and 24-48 hours after metronidazole (disulfiram-like reaction)."},
  {"topic":"STI","course":"D120","q":"A 21-year-old has trichomonas vaginitis. Treatment is prescribed. What is important to emphasize regarding partner treatment?","a":["Partners need not be treated since trichomonas is not an STI","Both partners MUST be treated simultaneously (sexual partners notified and treated) — trichomonas is an STI; reinfection rate is high without partner treatment","Only treat if partner has symptoms","Use topical clotrimazole for both partners"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Trichomonas vaginalis: STI caused by flagellated protozoa. BOTH partners must be treated simultaneously (metronidazole 2g single dose or 500mg BID x 7 days) to prevent reinfection. In HIV+ women, 500mg BID x 7 days is preferred. **Board tip:** Trichomonas is the most common curable non-viral STI in the U.S. Men are usually asymptomatic. Trichomonas in women: frothy yellow-green discharge, strawberry cervix (punctate hemorrhages), pH >4.5. Metronidazole resistance is increasing — tinidazole 2g single dose for treatment failures."},
  {"topic":"STI","course":"D120","q":"Which of the following is the MOST common bacterial STI in the United States?","a":["Gonorrhea","Syphilis","Chlamydia — ~1.6 million reported cases annually (the most common notifiable STI)","Trichomonas"],"correct":2,"rationale":"Chlamydia trachomatis is the most commonly reported notifiable disease in the United States. Approximately 1.6 million cases are reported annually, but true incidence is estimated at 4 million (due to significant underreporting from asymptomatic cases). Most cases are in young women 15-24 years old. **Board tip:** Chlamydia is called the 'silent epidemic' — 70-75% of infections in women are asymptomatic. This is why universal screening of sexually active women ≤24 is essential. Untreated: PID, infertility, ectopic pregnancy, reactive arthritis (Reiter's syndrome in males)."},
  {"topic":"STI","course":"D120","q":"HIV testing is recommended by USPSTF for which age group as a universal screen?","a":["Only persons who report IV drug use or MSM","All adolescents and adults aged 15-65 as a routine universal screen (Grade A recommendation); younger adolescents and older adults if at increased risk","Only persons with symptoms","Women only — men have lower HIV risk"],"correct":1,"rationale":"USPSTF Grade A: Screen for HIV in adolescents and adults aged 15-65, and in all pregnant women. Persons at high risk (MSM, PWID, sex workers) should be screened more frequently (at least annually). Early detection allows antiretroviral therapy (ART) that suppresses viral load to undetectable and prevents transmission (U=U: Undetectable = Untransmittable). **Board tip:** U=U principle: people with HIV on effective ART with sustained undetectable viral load have effectively zero risk of sexual transmission. This is a major public health message for reducing HIV stigma and encouraging testing."},
  {"topic":"STI","course":"D120","q":"A 17-year-old presents with a 1 cm painless genital ulcer. You are considering the differential. Which ulcer characteristic distinguishes syphilis from herpes and chancroid?","a":["All genital ulcers are painful","Syphilis chancre = painless, indurated, clean base, non-tender lymphadenopathy. Herpes = painful, multiple, vesicular → ulcers, tender lymphadenopathy. Chancroid = painful, purulent, undermined edges, tender suppurative lymphadenopathy (buboes)","All ulcers require biopsy","Herpes ulcers are always painless"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Genital ulcer differential: Syphilis (painless, firm, single, indurated) vs. Herpes (painful, multiple, preceded by vesicles) vs. Chancroid (H. ducreyi — painful with tender suppurative 'bubo' lymph nodes, rare in U.S.) vs. LGV (initial painless papule → painful inguinal lymphadenopathy). **Board tip:** In clinical practice, test for all three (RPR, HSV PCR, culture) because co-infection occurs. HIV testing always indicated with STI diagnosis. Donovanosis (granuloma inguinale): beefy-red painless ulcer, Klebsiella granulomatis, rare in U.S. — Donovan bodies on biopsy."},
  {"topic":"STI","course":"D120","q":"When treating secondary syphilis (diffuse rash including palms/soles, flu-like symptoms), what is the treatment and how does it differ from primary syphilis treatment?","a":["Oral doxycycline for secondary — IV penicillin for primary","Same treatment as primary: Benzathine penicillin G 2.4 million units IM single dose for primary AND secondary syphilis (early syphilis)","Doxycycline for 14 days instead of penicillin","Secondary syphilis requires 3 doses of Benzathine penicillin G (same as latent syphilis >1 year)"],"correct":0,"rationale":"Both primary and secondary (early) syphilis: Benzathine penicillin G 2.4 million units IM single dose is the treatment. Late latent or latent of unknown duration: 3 doses of Benzathine penicillin G weekly x 3 weeks. Neurosyphilis: IV aqueous penicillin G x 10-14 days. **Board tip:** Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction: fever, chills, headache 2-8 hours after penicillin treatment of early syphilis — not penicillin allergy; caused by cytokine release from spirochete killing. Treat with NSAIDs/acetaminophen. Warn patients especially if pregnant."},

  # ===== GERIATRICS (20) =====
  {"topic":"Geriatrics","course":"D120","q":"An 80-year-old man has hypertension, atrial fibrillation, osteoarthritis, GERD, depression, and insomnia. He is on amlodipine, warfarin, naproxen, omeprazole, mirtazapine, and diphenhydramine (Benadryl). Which medication should be immediately discontinued per BEERS Criteria?","a":["Amlodipine","Diphenhydramine (Benadryl) — antihistamines are strongly anticholinergic, cause confusion, sedation, falls, urinary retention in elderly; BEERS Class A — avoid in older adults","Omeprazole","Warfarin"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Beers Criteria (AGS): Diphenhydramine/antihistamines are STRONGLY anticholinergic medications to AVOID in older adults — causes confusion, excessive sedation, falls, urinary retention, constipation. **Board tip:** Other BEERS medications to avoid in elderly: benzodiazepines (fall risk, cognitive impairment), non-BZD hypnotics (Z-drugs — zolpidem), tricyclic antidepressants, muscle relaxants (cyclobenzaprine), anticholinergic bladder drugs, NSAIDs (GI bleed risk, fluid retention), meperidine, antipsychotics (stroke risk). The 'START/STOPP' criteria is another useful tool."},
  {"topic":"Geriatrics","course":"D120","q":"Which validated tool is recommended by USPSTF for screening cognitive impairment in older adults and consists of a 30-question, 10-minute test assessing orientation, memory, language, and attention?","a":["PHQ-9","Mini-Mental State Examination (MMSE) — 30 points total, widely used, scores: 24-30 normal, 18-23 mild, 12-17 moderate, <12 severe impairment","Montreal Cognitive Assessment (MoCA) — 30 points, more sensitive for mild cognitive impairment","Confusion Assessment Method (CAM)"],"correct":1,"rationale":"MMSE: 30-point test assessing: orientation (10 pts), registration/recall (6 pts), attention/calculation (5 pts), language (8 pts), visuospatial (1 pt). Score <24 = cognitive impairment. SENSITIVITY for dementia: ~70-90%. **Board tip:** MoCA is MORE SENSITIVE than MMSE for mild cognitive impairment (MCI) — better at detecting early dementia in high-functioning individuals (college-educated). Both are used in practice: MMSE for following progression, MoCA for initial MCI screening. USPSTF states evidence is insufficient to recommend for or against universal dementia screening (2020)."},
  {"topic":"Geriatrics","course":"D120","q":"A 78-year-old woman has fallen twice in the past 6 months. What is the comprehensive falls risk assessment approach?","a":["X-ray only to evaluate for fractures","Comprehensive assessment: medication review (polypharmacy), orthostatic BP, vision testing, gait and balance assessment (Timed Up and Go test), cognitive screen, home environment assessment, bone density for fracture risk","Tell her to use a cane and fall prevention is complete","Refer to orthopedics only"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Falls risk assessment: USPSTF recommends exercise interventions for community-dwelling adults ≥65 with fall risk. Comprehensive multifactorial intervention: TUG test (≥12 seconds = high risk), orthostatic hypotension, medication review (eliminate Beers medications), vision correction, vitamin D supplementation, home safety assessment, and referral to PT for balance/strength training. **Board tip:** Timed Up and Go (TUG) test: >12 seconds = high fall risk. Vitamin D 800 IU/day reduces falls in deficient older adults. Major fall risk medications: benzodiazepines, antipsychotics, antihypertensives (orthostasis), sleep aids, anticholinergics."},
  {"topic":"Geriatrics","course":"D120","q":"An 82-year-old patient with multiple comorbidities is on 11 medications. She presents with new confusion, falls, and urinary incontinence. What clinical approach addresses this?","a":["Add medications to treat each new symptom separately","Conduct a medication reconciliation (brown bag review) — drug-drug interactions, cumulative anticholinergic burden, and unnecessary medications are likely contributing to new symptoms (polypharmacy cascade)","Assume these are new diseases and order extensive workup only","Refer to specialist for each symptom separately"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Polypharmacy (≥5 medications): very common in older adults, increases risk of adverse drug reactions, drug-drug interactions, falls, cognitive impairment, hospitalization. 'Prescribing cascade': new medication prescribed to treat side effect of another — cascade of new prescriptions. **Board tip:** Approach: Conduct 'brown bag' medication review (patient brings all medications), identify and deprescribe inappropriate medications using BEERS or STOPP criteria, review OTC medications and supplements (often overlooked). In geriatrics, less is often more — deprescribing is a therapeutic intervention."},
  {"topic":"Geriatrics","course":"D120","q":"A 75-year-old woman is diagnosed with Alzheimer's dementia (mild stage). What pharmacologic treatment is FDA-approved for this stage?","a":["Memantine (Namenda) — approved for moderate-severe Alzheimer's only","Donepezil (Aricept) — cholinesterase inhibitor approved for all stages of Alzheimer's; modest symptomatic benefit","No FDA-approved medications exist for early Alzheimer's","Amyloid antibody therapy only for late-stage"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Alzheimer's disease treatment: Cholinesterase inhibitors (donepezil, rivastigmine, galantamine) for mild-moderate AD. Memantine for moderate-severe. Combination (donepezil + memantine) for moderate-severe. Lecanemab (Leqembi) and donanemab: new anti-amyloid monoclonal antibodies FDA-approved for early/mild AD — modest disease modification. **Board tip:** Cholinesterase inhibitors: mechanism = inhibit acetylcholinesterase → more acetylcholine available (compensates for neuron loss). Side effects: nausea/vomiting, diarrhea, bradycardia (use caution in cardiac disease). Benefit is modest but real — treatment should not be withheld."},
  {"topic":"Geriatrics","course":"D120","q":"Which screening test does USPSTF recommend for abdominal aortic aneurysm (AAA) and in which population?","a":["Annual CT scan for all adults over 60","One-time screening abdominal ultrasound for men aged 65-75 who have ever smoked (≥100 lifetime cigarettes) — Grade B recommendation","Annual abdominal ultrasound for all adults over 65","No screening recommended — AAA is symptomatic when it ruptures"],"correct":1,"rationale":"USPSTF Grade B: One-time abdominal ultrasound screening for AAA in men aged 65-75 who have EVER smoked (≥100 cigarettes lifetime). AAA is largely asymptomatic until rupture (which has >90% mortality). Screening ultrasound is safe, cost-effective, and reduces AAA-related mortality. **Board tip:** AAA surveillance intervals: <3 cm = no surveillance; 3-3.9 cm = rescreen in 3 years; 4-4.9 cm = every 12 months; ≥5 cm = surgical referral. Surgical repair threshold: ≥5.5 cm in men, ≥5 cm in women, or rapid growth >0.5 cm in 6 months."},
  {"topic":"Geriatrics","course":"D120","q":"An 80-year-old man has blood pressure 158/88. He has no symptoms and is currently on no medications. What is the current evidence-based approach to his hypertension?","a":["No treatment needed — blood pressure over 140 is normal at age 80","The SPRINT trial supports treating systolic BP to a goal of <130 mmHg even in adults ≥75 with good functional status; balance benefits against risks (falls, orthostatic hypotension). Individualize goals based on frailty.","Aggressive treatment to 120/70 is the goal in all elderly","Only treat if BP is >180/120"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Hypertension in elderly: AHA/ACC guidelines recommend BP target <130/80 for most adults (including fit elderly ≥65). SPRINT trial showed intensive control (SBP <120) reduced cardiovascular events and mortality in adults ≥75 with high CV risk. **Board tip:** In FRAIL elderly: less aggressive targets may be appropriate (individualize). Orthostatic hypotension and falls risk must be balanced against CV benefit. Start low, go slow. Preferred agents in elderly: thiazide diuretics, CCBs, ACE inhibitors/ARBs. Avoid high-dose beta-blockers as first-line."},
  {"topic":"Geriatrics","course":"D120","q":"A 77-year-old woman is brought in by her daughter due to confusion that developed over 24 hours. She had a UTI treated last week, is on multiple medications, and seems 'not herself.' Mini-Cog shows significant impairment. What is the most likely diagnosis?","a":["New Alzheimer's dementia","Delirium — acute, fluctuating confusion with a likely precipitating cause (UTI, medications, dehydration) — NOT dementia. Treat the underlying cause.","Stroke — MRI brain stat for all acute confusion","Normal aging changes"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Delirium: Acute onset, fluctuating course, inattention + disorganized thinking or altered consciousness. Key distinguishing feature from dementia: ACUTE onset and FLUCTUATING course. Precipitants (DELIRIUM mnemonic): Drugs, Electrolyte abnormalities, Lack of drugs (withdrawal), Infection, Reduced sensory input, Intracranial, Urinary/fecal retention, Myocardial/metabolic. **Board tip:** CAM (Confusion Assessment Method) is the gold-standard tool to diagnose delirium: acute onset + fluctuating course + inattention + either disorganized thinking OR altered consciousness. Delirium has high mortality (25-33%) in hospitalized elderly."},
  {"topic":"Geriatrics","course":"D120","q":"An 82-year-old man reports urinary urgency, frequency, and nocturia 3x/night. This is affecting his sleep and quality of life. He denies incontinence. What is the first-line management for overactive bladder (OAB)?","a":["Oxybutynin (Ditropan) immediately — most effective anticholinergic","Bladder training, scheduled voiding, pelvic floor exercises (Kegel), and fluid management — behavioral interventions are FIRST-LINE; pharmacotherapy if these fail (prefer beta-3 agonist mirabegron over anticholinergics in elderly)","Intermittent catheterization","Refer to urology immediately"],"correct":1,"rationale":"OAB management: First-line = behavioral therapy (bladder training, voiding schedule, fluid management, pelvic floor exercises). If pharmacotherapy needed: prefer MIRABEGRON (beta-3 adrenergic agonist) over anticholinergics (oxybutynin, tolterodine) in elderly — anticholinergics increase dementia risk and are on BEERS list. **Board tip:** BEERS criteria strongly advises AGAINST anticholinergic bladder agents (oxybutynin, tolterodine, solifenacin, darifenacin, fesoterodine) in adults ≥65. Mirabegron is preferred: no anticholinergic effects, safe in elderly. Botulinum toxin injection or sacral nerve modulation for refractory OAB."},
  {"topic":"Geriatrics","course":"D120","q":"A 72-year-old woman has recently lost her husband of 45 years and presents with sadness, poor appetite, difficulty sleeping, and occasional thoughts that 'life is not worth living.' She denies active suicidal ideation. How should this be approached?","a":["Grief is normal — no intervention needed, come back in 6 months","Assess for major depressive disorder (complicated grief) using PHQ-9; grief can trigger MDD requiring treatment. Provide bereavement support, consider therapy, and treat MDD if criteria met. Elderly suicide risk is high — reassess regularly.","Prescribe benzodiazepine for sleep","Refer to hospice"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Grief vs. MDD: Grief is normal but can trigger major depressive disorder (MDD). Distinguish: grief is focused on loss, waves of sadness, may improve. MDD: persistent depressed mood for ≥2 weeks, anhedonia, affecting function. Elderly men are the highest-risk group for suicide completion. PHQ-9 screening essential. **Board tip:** Elderly depression is frequently undetected. PHQ-9 is validated for elderly. Treatment: psychotherapy (CBT, grief therapy) + antidepressant (SSRIs preferred in elderly — sertraline, escitalopram). Avoid TCAs in elderly (cardiac side effects, anticholinergic effects)."},
  {"topic":"Geriatrics","course":"D120","q":"An 85-year-old woman with advanced dementia is admitted with aspiration pneumonia — her third hospitalization in 6 months. Her daughter, listed as healthcare proxy, insists on 'everything done.' What is the appropriate response?","a":["Always do everything requested by family","Explore the patient's previously expressed wishes (advance directives), discuss goals of care with the daughter (distinguishing between life-sustaining treatments vs. comfort-focused care), and clarify what 'everything' means to the family","Document 'full code' and move on","Immediately initiate hospice without discussing with family"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Goals of care conversations: Essential in geriatric primary care. Key elements: review advance directives/POLST, clarify patient's previously expressed values, educate family about disease trajectory (advanced dementia is a terminal illness), discuss burdens vs. benefits of interventions (hospitalization, tube feeding — evidence shows harm in advanced dementia). **Board tip:** Tube feeding in advanced dementia: Multiple RCTs show tube feeding does NOT improve survival, prevent aspiration, improve quality of life, or reduce pressure ulcers. Careful hand feeding is preferred. This is a critical, board-tested fact about advanced dementia management."},
  {"topic":"Geriatrics","course":"D120","q":"A 70-year-old man with new diagnosis of type 2 diabetes has an A1C of 8.1%. What is the appropriate A1C target for this patient?","a":["<6.5% — tight control for all diabetics","A1C <7.5-8% is an appropriate target for older adults with moderate life expectancy and multiple comorbidities — less stringent targets reduce hypoglycemia risk (which is especially dangerous in elderly)","<5.7% — normal level is the goal","<9% — only treat if A1C is above 9%"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Diabetes management in elderly: ADA recommends individualized A1C targets. Healthy elderly (good functional status, long life expectancy): <7-7.5%. Complex/intermediate (multiple chronic conditions, early cognitive impairment): <8%. Very complex/poor health: <8.5-9%. **Board tip:** In elderly, hypoglycemia is MORE dangerous than in younger adults — falls, cardiac events, cognitive deterioration. Avoid sulfonylureas in elderly (hypoglycemia risk). Preferred agents: metformin (if tolerated), SGLT2i (CV benefit), GLP-1 agonist, or basal insulin with careful titration."},
  {"topic":"Geriatrics","course":"D120","q":"The FRAIL scale assesses frailty in older adults. Which five components does it measure?","a":["Falls, Renal function, Age, Illness, Loss of weight","Fatigue, Resistance (ability to climb stairs), Ambulation (ability to walk a block), Illness (5+ comorbidities), Loss of weight (>5%)","Fasting glucose, Renal function, Anemia, Inflammation, Low albumin","Fear, Resistance to care, Activity limitation, Irritability, Loss of memory"],"correct":1,"rationale":"FRAIL scale: Fatigue, Resistance (stairs), Ambulation (blocks), Illness (≥5 chronic conditions), Loss of weight (>5%). Score: 0 = robust; 1-2 = pre-frail; 3-5 = frail. Frailty is associated with adverse health outcomes (hospitalization, falls, disability, mortality). **Board tip:** Fried Frailty Phenotype (more complex): weakness, slow walking speed, exhaustion, low physical activity, unintentional weight loss. Any 3+ = frail. 1-2 = pre-frail. Frailty assessment should influence treatment decisions and goals of care."},
  {"topic":"Geriatrics","course":"D120","q":"An 80-year-old woman is newly prescribed a proton pump inhibitor (PPI) for GERD. What long-term monitoring concern should be addressed?","a":["PPIs cause kidney failure after 1 month","Long-term PPI use (>1 year) is associated with: magnesium deficiency, vitamin B12 deficiency, Clostridioides difficile infection risk, bone fracture risk (calcium absorption), and hyponatremia — use lowest effective dose and reassess need annually","PPIs cause cataracts","PPIs increase cardiac risk in elderly"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Long-term PPI concerns: hypomagnesemia (monitor Mg2+ in patients on diuretics or digoxin), vitamin B12 deficiency (reduced absorption), C. diff risk, osteoporosis/hip fracture (impaired calcium absorption). In elderly: use lowest effective dose, 'step-down' to H2 blocker or PRN use when possible. **Board tip:** PPIs are one of the most overprescribed medication classes. Many patients prescribed PPIs in hospital continue indefinitely without indication. Reassess annually: is there an active indication? Can the patient use H2 blocker instead? Appropriate long-term use: Barrett's esophagus, severe GERD, prophylaxis during NSAID use."},
  {"topic":"Geriatrics","course":"D120","q":"Urinary incontinence (UI) affects 30-60% of older women. Which type presents as involuntary leakage with cough, sneeze, or physical activity?","a":["Urge incontinence — strong urge precedes leakage","Stress incontinence — weakened pelvic floor muscles/urethral sphincter allow leakage with increased intra-abdominal pressure. Treatment: pelvic floor muscle exercises (Kegel), weight loss, pessary","Overflow incontinence — from bladder overdistension","Functional incontinence — inability to reach toilet in time"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Stress urinary incontinence: Most common type in younger and middle-aged women. Caused by pelvic floor laxity, urethral hypermobility from childbirth, obesity, aging. Leakage with cough, sneeze, exercise, laugh. Treatment: Kegel exercises (PFMT), weight loss, duloxetine (off-label), or surgical sling. **Board tip:** Incontinence types: Stress = leaks with physical activity; Urge = OAB, strong urge; Overflow = continuous dribbling, large post-void residual (benign prostatic hyperplasia in men); Functional = immobility, cognitive impairment prevents toileting. Mixed UI (stress + urge) is most common in older women."},
  {"topic":"Geriatrics","course":"D120","q":"An older adult patient mentions she has been feeling isolated and lonely since her husband died and her children moved away. What is the health impact of social isolation in older adults?","a":["Social isolation has no clinically significant health effects","Social isolation in older adults is associated with equivalent mortality risk to smoking 15 cigarettes/day, and is linked to increased risk of dementia (50% increase), depression, cardiovascular disease, and premature death — address at every visit","Social isolation only affects mental health, not physical health","Loneliness is a normal part of aging — no intervention needed"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Social isolation and loneliness: Recognized as a major public health concern (U.S. Surgeon General advisory 2023). Associated with: 50% increased dementia risk, 29% increased heart disease, 32% increased stroke risk, and mortality equivalent to smoking. Interventions: community programs, senior centers, telehealth for isolated patients, faith communities, volunteer programs. **Board tip:** Screen for social isolation at geriatric visits (UCLA Loneliness Scale or simple questions). Address at every visit. Refer to community resources. Social prescribing (connecting patients to community activities) is an emerging evidence-based approach."},
  {"topic":"Geriatrics","course":"D120","q":"An 85-year-old man on warfarin (INR therapeutic at 2.3) for atrial fibrillation needs a hip replacement. His CHA2DS2-VASc score is 5. What is the appropriate perioperative management?","a":["Stop warfarin 24 hours before surgery — no bridging needed","Hold warfarin 5 days before surgery; bridge with LMWH (enoxaparin) if high thromboembolic risk (CHA2DS2-VASc ≥5). Discuss risk/benefit of perioperative bridging (increased bleeding risk vs. stroke prevention) with surgical team.","Continue warfarin throughout surgery unchanged","Switch to aspirin permanently"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Perioperative anticoagulation: Hold warfarin 5 days before major surgery. CHA2DS2-VASc ≥5 (high thromboembolic risk) may warrant LMWH bridging — but recent studies (BRIDGE trial) showed bridging in AFIB did NOT reduce thromboembolism but INCREASED bleeding. Individualize decision with surgical team. **Board tip:** CHA2DS2-VASc score: CHF (1), Hypertension (1), Age ≥75 (2), Diabetes (1), Stroke/TIA (2), Vascular disease (1), Age 65-74 (1), Sex Category female (1). Score ≥2 in men, ≥3 in women = anticoagulation recommended."},
  {"topic":"Geriatrics","course":"D120","q":"A 68-year-old woman asks about vaccines recommended for older adults. Which vaccines are recommended for all adults ≥65?","a":["Hepatitis B and Tdap only","Annual influenza (inactivated), COVID-19 (updated annually), high-dose or adjuvanted influenza vaccine preferred ≥65, pneumococcal (PCV20 or PCV15+PPSV23 series), Tdap (if not previously vaccinated), zoster (Shingrix — 2-dose recombinant at age 50+)","Varicella booster and MMR booster","No additional vaccines — childhood vaccines are sufficient"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Vaccines for adults ≥65: Annual flu (high-dose Fluzone or adjuvanted Fluad preferred ≥65 — 4x antibody response), COVID-19 (updated annually), Pneumococcal (PCV20 preferred — one dose, or PCV15 followed by PPSV23), Shingrix (recombinant zoster vaccine 2 doses 2-6 months apart, starting at age 50 — 97% efficacy ≥50 years), and Tdap if not previously given as adult. **Board tip:** Shingrix (RZV) is >97% effective vs. 51% for Zostavax (no longer available in U.S.). Shingrix replaces Zostavax. Even if had natural shingles or received Zostavax — still get Shingrix. Pneumococcal: PCV20 (Prevnar 20) is now the preferred single-dose option."},
  {"topic":"Geriatrics","course":"D120","q":"What is the MOST important single intervention to reduce the overall burden of disease in older adults, according to geriatric population health research?","a":["Prescribing more medications to control individual diseases","Advance care planning (ACP) — discussing and documenting patient goals, values, and preferences for future medical care (POLST/MOLST forms, healthcare proxy designation) — reduces unwanted aggressive interventions, reduces hospitalization, and improves quality of life and death","Annual cardiac stress testing","Routine full-body skin examination"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Advance care planning (ACP): Discussing and documenting goals of care, naming a healthcare proxy, and completing POLST (Portable Medical Orders for Life-Sustaining Treatment) significantly improves end-of-life care quality, reduces unwanted aggressive interventions, and is associated with better quality of death. **Board tip:** POLST/MOLST is a medical order (signed by provider) — valid across healthcare settings. Different from advance directive (legal document). Every provider caring for adults ≥65 or with serious illness should initiate ACP conversations. Ask at every annual visit: 'Have you thought about who would make medical decisions for you if you couldn't?'"},
  {"topic":"Geriatrics","course":"D120","q":"A 74-year-old man has a serum creatinine of 1.4 mg/dL. His estimated GFR (CKD-EPI) is 42 mL/min. How should this affect his medication management?","a":["This is normal for his age — no medication adjustment needed","eGFR 30-44 = CKD Stage 3b — dose-adjust renally cleared medications (metformin hold if eGFR <30, antibiotics adjust doses, NSAIDs avoid, SGLT2i reduce efficacy <45); monitor renal function every 3-6 months","Start dialysis immediately","No medication adjustments needed until dialysis is required"],"correct":1,"rationale":"CKD staging and medication management: eGFR 42 = CKD Stage 3b. Key medication adjustments: metformin (hold if eGFR <30, use caution 30-45), NSAIDs (avoid — worsen CKD), SGLT2i (efficacy reduced below eGFR 45), direct oral anticoagulants (dose adjust by drug and eGFR), aminoglycosides (avoid), antibiotics (dose adjust many). **Board tip:** Many elderly patients have 'normal' serum creatinine with reduced GFR due to decreased muscle mass (less creatinine production). ALWAYS calculate eGFR using CKD-EPI formula — do NOT rely on creatinine alone in elderly. This is why Cockcroft-Gault formula is also used for medication dosing."},

  # ===== MEN'S HEALTH (15) =====
  {"topic":"Men's Health","course":"D120","q":"A 55-year-old man has a PSA of 5.2 ng/mL. He is asymptomatic. He is interested in prostate cancer screening. What is the current USPSTF recommendation on PSA screening?","a":["PSA screening is absolutely contraindicated — causes more harm than good","For men aged 55-69: shared decision-making — discuss benefits (early detection) and harms (false positives, biopsy complications, overtreatment) of PSA screening. For men ≥70: recommend AGAINST routine PSA screening (Grade D).","PSA screening is mandatory annually for all men ≥40","PSA ≤4.0 ng/mL is always normal — no further action needed"],"correct":1,"rationale":"USPSTF (2018): PSA screening for men 55-69: Grade C — shared decision-making. Men ≥70: Grade D — recommend against. PSA has significant limitations: many false positives leading to unnecessary biopsies (complications: bleeding, infection, erectile dysfunction, incontinence), and overdiagnosis of low-risk cancers. **Board tip:** PSA >4 ng/mL often prompts biopsy but is NOT diagnostic alone. PSA velocity (rapid rise over time) and free PSA % are additional considerations. Refer to urology for PSA 4-10 ng/mL or PSA velocity >0.75 ng/mL/year for consideration of biopsy."},
  {"topic":"Men's Health","course":"D120","q":"A 52-year-old man complains of decreased libido, erectile dysfunction, fatigue, and loss of muscle mass. Total testosterone is 220 ng/dL (normal >300). What is the diagnosis and treatment?","a":["Normal aging — testosterone varies; no treatment warranted","Hypogonadism — confirm with two morning testosterone levels (<300 ng/dL) + LH/FSH; treat with testosterone replacement therapy (TRT) — topical gel, patch, or IM injection; monitor hemoglobin, PSA, LH/FSH","Treat with SSRIs for depression","Start PDE-5 inhibitor for ED only"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Male hypogonadism diagnosis: Two morning total testosterone levels <300 ng/dL (labs should be 8-10am as testosterone peaks in the morning) + symptoms. Causes: primary (hypergonadotropic — elevated LH/FSH, testicular failure) vs. secondary (hypogonadotropic — low/normal LH/FSH, hypothalamic-pituitary cause). TRT forms: topical gel (daily), IM testosterone cypionate or enanthate (every 2 weeks), or subcutaneous pellets. **Board tip:** TRT monitoring: testosterone level (goal: mid-normal range), hematocrit (polycythemia risk), PSA, DRE. Contraindications: prostate cancer, breast cancer, polycythemia, untreated sleep apnea. TRT causes azoospermia — discuss fertility if patient desires children."},
  {"topic":"Men's Health","course":"D120","q":"A 64-year-old man has BPH with lower urinary tract symptoms (LUTS): urinary frequency, weak stream, hesitancy, and nocturia 3x/night. IPSS score is 18 (moderate). He has no obstructive uropathy. What is the initial pharmacologic treatment?","a":["Finasteride alone (5-alpha reductase inhibitor) for all BPH","Alpha-1 blocker (tamsulosin, alfuzosin, silodosin) for quick symptom relief — work within days; combine with 5-ARI (finasteride) for large prostate (>40g) or high PSA for additive benefit","Antibiotics — BPH is caused by infection","Immediate transurethral resection of the prostate (TURP)"],"correct":1,"rationale":"BPH pharmacotherapy: Alpha-1 blockers (tamsulosin, alfuzosin, silodosin) relax smooth muscle in prostate/bladder neck — work within days, don't reduce prostate size. 5-ARI (finasteride, dutasteride) reduce prostate size 20-30% over 6 months — better for large prostates, reduce progression. Combination (alpha-blocker + 5-ARI) for moderate-severe symptoms with large prostate. **Board tip:** Tamsulosin side effects: retrograde ejaculation (urine-ejaculate mixing), orthostatic hypotension. 5-ARI side effects: sexual dysfunction (decreased libido, ED), gynecomastia; also REDUCE PSA by 50% (adjust interpretation). TURP is gold-standard surgical treatment for refractory BPH."},
  {"topic":"Men's Health","course":"D120","q":"A 45-year-old man requests help for erectile dysfunction (ED). He smokes, has hypertension and hyperlipidemia on amlodipine and atorvastatin. What is the first step in evaluation?","a":["Prescribe sildenafil (Viagra) immediately","Evaluate for cardiovascular risk — ED is often an early marker of atherosclerosis/endothelial dysfunction. Check fasting glucose, lipids, testosterone, PSA. Lifestyle modification (smoking cessation, exercise) before or alongside PDE-5 inhibitor therapy.","Refer to urology only","Testosterone testing is the first step for all ED patients"],"correct":1,"rationale":"ED evaluation: IMPORTANT — ED is a cardiovascular risk equivalent. Men with new ED have 3x higher risk of future cardiac event. Evaluate: cardiovascular risk factors (HTN, DM, smoking, dyslipidemia), testosterone (low T causes ED), psychosocial factors, medication side effects (beta-blockers, antidepressants, antihypertensives cause ED). **Board tip:** PDE-5 inhibitors (sildenafil/Viagra, tadalafil/Cialis, vardenafil): first-line for ED. Absolute contraindication: nitrates (causes severe hypotension). Tadalafil 5mg daily is also FDA-approved for BPH (dual benefit for men with both conditions)."},
  {"topic":"Men's Health","course":"D120","q":"Testicular cancer is the most common solid tumor in men aged 15-35. Which examination is recommended for early detection?","a":["Annual PSA testing in young men","Monthly testicular self-examination (TSE) is recommended for all young men — soft, painless swelling of a testicle, new hardness, or heaviness warrants immediate evaluation with testicular ultrasound","Annual CT chest/abdomen","USPSTF recommends against routine TSE — testicular cancer screening has net harm"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Testicular cancer: Rare overall but leading cancer in males 15-35. Classic presentation: painless testicular swelling or mass. Risk factors: cryptorchidism (10x increased risk), Klinefelter syndrome, family history. Evaluation: testicular ultrasound (first-line imaging), tumor markers (AFP, beta-hCG, LDH). Treatment: orchiectomy (inguinal approach). **Board tip:** USPSTF states insufficient evidence for routine TSE screening in asymptomatic adolescents/adults. However, education about TSE and prompt evaluation of testicular symptoms is widely recommended. Any testicular mass = ultrasound first (never biopsy through scrotal approach — risk of lymphatic spread to inguinal nodes)."},
  {"topic":"Men's Health","course":"D120","q":"A 62-year-old man with a 45-pack-year smoking history currently smokes 1 ppd. He is worried about lung cancer. What screening does USPSTF recommend?","a":["Chest X-ray annually — effective and low-cost","Annual low-dose CT (LDCT) chest for adults aged 50-80 who have a 20-pack-year history and currently smoke or quit within the past 15 years (USPSTF 2021, Grade B)","Sputum cytology","No lung cancer screening is effective"],"correct":1,"rationale":"USPSTF (2021) lung cancer screening: Annual LDCT for adults 50-80 years with ≥20 pack-year history who currently smoke OR quit within past 15 years. NLST and NELSON trials: LDCT reduces lung cancer mortality by 20-24%. Discontinue screening if patient has not smoked for >15 years or develops health condition limiting life expectancy. **Board tip:** Updated 2021 criteria expanded from 2013 (age 55-80, 30 pack-years) to age 50-80 and 20 pack-years. This significantly increases screening eligibility. Smoking cessation counseling must accompany screening discussions."},
  {"topic":"Men's Health","course":"D120","q":"A 58-year-old man with hyperlipidemia asks about his cardiovascular risk. He has no history of CVD events. Total cholesterol 220, LDL 145, HDL 38, BP 134/82, and non-smoker. According to ASCVD risk calculator, his 10-year risk is 9%. What is the management?","a":["No statin needed — risk is below 10%","Borderline risk (7.5-10%): shared decision-making; consider risk-enhancing factors. ACC/AHA threshold for statin therapy in primary prevention: ≥10% 10-year ASCVD risk (moderate-high intensity statin). At 9%, discuss lifestyle modification and consider statin if risk-enhancing factors present.","Immediately start high-intensity statin","Start aspirin for primary prevention"],"correct":1,"rationale":"ACC/AHA 2019 guidelines: Statin therapy in primary prevention for adults with LDL ≥70 mg/dL: High-risk (≥20%): high-intensity statin. Intermediate risk (7.5-19.9%): moderate-intensity statin recommended. Borderline (5-7.4%): consider statin with risk-enhancing factors. Low (<5%): lifestyle only. **Board tip:** Risk-enhancing factors that favor statin: family history premature ASCVD, LDL ≥160, metabolic syndrome, CKD, elevated hsCRP, elevated Lp(a), ABI <0.9. If uncertain: coronary artery calcium (CAC) scoring can help guide decision. Aspirin for primary prevention: USPSTF 2022 recommends AGAINST initiating aspirin for primary CVD prevention in adults ≥60 years."},
  {"topic":"Men's Health","course":"D120","q":"A 50-year-old man is diagnosed with obstructive sleep apnea (OSA) after polysomnography shows AHI of 28 (moderate-severe). What is the primary treatment?","a":["Weight loss alone is curative — no device needed","Continuous positive airway pressure (CPAP) therapy — gold standard treatment for moderate-severe OSA. Reduces AHI, improves daytime sleepiness, reduces cardiovascular risk (hypertension, atrial fibrillation, stroke).","Positional therapy only (sleep on side)","Uvulopalatopharyngoplasty (UPPP) as first-line surgery"],"correct":1,"rationale":"OSA management: CPAP is the gold standard for moderate-severe OSA (AHI ≥15). Benefits: resolves hypoxemia, reduces daytime sleepiness, reduces cardiovascular risk, improves insulin sensitivity. Alternatives for mild OSA or CPAP intolerance: mandibular advancement device, positional therapy (positional OSA), weight loss (if obese). **Board tip:** CPAP compliance (≥4 hours/night ≥70% of nights) is the key challenge. Adherence assessment at follow-up visits. OSA is strongly associated with: obesity, hypertension (resistant HTN), atrial fibrillation, type 2 diabetes, nocturnal angina. Screen with STOP-BANG questionnaire."},
  {"topic":"Men's Health","course":"D120","q":"A 48-year-old man is found to have chlamydia during an STI screen. His last sexual contact was a female partner 2 weeks ago. Per public health reporting requirements, what must occur?","a":["Patient confidentiality prohibits any reporting","Chlamydia is a mandatorily reportable STI — report to local/state health department. Provide partner notification services (or give patient expedited partner therapy — EPT — where legal) to treat all recent partners.","Only report if the patient has symptoms","Report only to the patient's primary care physician"],"correct":1,"rationale":"STI reporting: Chlamydia, gonorrhea, syphilis, HIV, and other STIs are MANDATORILY reportable to local/state health departments. Partner notification (contact tracing) is essential to prevent ongoing transmission. EPT (Expedited Partner Therapy): provider gives prescription/medication for patient to deliver to partner — legal in most states, reduces reinfection. **Board tip:** Healthcare providers are legally required to report specific communicable diseases. Failure to report is both a legal and public health failure. Patient confidentiality is maintained through anonymous reporting protocols. EPT is particularly important for gonorrhea and chlamydia — studies show it reduces reinfection rates."},
  {"topic":"Men's Health","course":"D120","q":"Which lifestyle modification has the MOST impact on improving erectile dysfunction in men who are overweight and physically inactive?","a":["Dietary supplements (saw palmetto, zinc)","Regular aerobic exercise and weight loss — shown in multiple RCTs to improve erectile function significantly, comparable to pharmacotherapy in men with metabolic syndrome and obesity","Testosterone supplementation immediately","Daily low-dose aspirin"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Exercise for ED: Aerobic exercise 40 minutes, 4x/week significantly improves erectile function in overweight men, those with CVD, hypertension, and metabolic syndrome. Weight loss of 10% body weight improves testosterone levels and ED. Exercise reduces endothelial dysfunction — the underlying vascular mechanism of ED in most men. **Board tip:** Address cardiovascular risk factors FIRST in men with ED — hypertension, dyslipidemia, smoking, diabetes, and physical inactivity are all treatable contributors. ED is a 'canary in the coal mine' for cardiovascular disease (penile vessels are smaller than coronary arteries — symptoms appear first in penis, then heart)."},
  {"topic":"Men's Health","course":"D120","q":"A 45-year-old male athlete is using anabolic androgenic steroids (AAS) purchased online for muscle building. What are the serious medical consequences he should be counseled about?","a":["AAS are safe when used under physician supervision only — no risks","AAS abuse causes: testicular atrophy and azoospermia (temporary or permanent infertility), hypogonadism after cessation, left ventricular hypertrophy (cardiomyopathy), elevated hematocrit (thrombosis risk), dyslipidemia, liver toxicity (oral AAS), acne, and psychiatric effects (roid rage, depression)","Only weight gain is a concern","Anabolic steroids are too weak to cause harm"],"correct":1,"rationale":"AAS abuse consequences: Reproductive (testicular atrophy, azoospermia, reduced LH/FSH), Cardiovascular (LVH, dilated cardiomyopathy, sudden death, dyslipidemia — decreased HDL, elevated LDL), Hematologic (erythrocytosis), Hepatic (oral AAS cause peliosis hepatis, hepatocellular carcinoma), Dermatologic (severe acne, alopecia), Psychiatric (aggression, mood swings, depression with cessation). **Board tip:** Performance-enhancing drug use is common but underreported. Ask non-judgmentally in sports physicals. AAS are Schedule III controlled substances. Post-AAS hypogonadism: suppressed HPG axis needs months to years to recover."},
  {"topic":"Men's Health","course":"D120","q":"A 38-year-old man and his partner of 3 years are having difficulty conceiving after 12 months of unprotected intercourse. What is the evaluation for male factor infertility?","a":["Semen analysis is the only test ever needed","Semen analysis is the first-line test: ≥15 million sperm/mL, ≥40% motility, ≥4% normal morphology (WHO 2010). If abnormal: repeat in 4-6 weeks, hormonal panel (FSH, LH, testosterone, prolactin), scrotal ultrasound, referral to reproductive urology.","Chromosome karyotype first","Testicular biopsy first"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Male infertility evaluation: Semen analysis is the cornerstone (WHO 2010 reference values: concentration ≥16M/mL, motility ≥42%, morphology ≥4% normal). Male factor contributes to ~50% of infertility cases (alone or combined). If abnormal: repeat in 4-6 weeks (significant day-to-day variability), then hormonal evaluation (azoospermia + high FSH = primary testicular failure; azoospermia + low FSH = hypogonadotropic hypogonadism — treatable). **Board tip:** Male infertility causes: varicocele (most common correctable cause), cryptorchidism, Klinefelter syndrome (47,XXY), hormonal (low T, elevated prolactin), genetic (Y chromosome microdeletions), obstructive (vasectomy, cystic fibrosis-related absence of vas deferens)."},
  {"topic":"Men's Health","course":"D120","q":"Which of the following is the most common cancer in American men?","a":["Lung cancer","Prostate cancer — most common cancer in American men (excluding skin cancer); second leading cause of cancer death in men (after lung cancer)","Colorectal cancer","Bladder cancer"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Prostate cancer: Most common cancer in American men (estimated 300,000 new cases/year) but NOT the leading cause of cancer death (lung cancer kills more). Most prostate cancers are slow-growing and indolent. Gleason score/ISUP grade determines aggressiveness. **Board tip:** Prostate cancer racial disparity: Black men have 2x higher incidence and 2x higher mortality than white men — likely start PSA screening conversations at age 40-45 for Black men. Family history of prostate, breast, or ovarian cancer (BRCA1/2 mutations) also increases risk and warrants earlier discussion."},
  {"topic":"Men's Health","course":"D120","q":"A 35-year-old man presents with painful scrotal swelling, dysuria, and urethral discharge. He is sexually active with multiple partners. Exam shows epididymis tenderness. What is the most likely cause and treatment?","a":["Testicular torsion — immediate surgical referral","Epididymitis/orchitis — likely sexually transmitted (Chlamydia trachomatis, Neisseria gonorrhoeae in sexually active men <35). Treat: ceftriaxone 500mg IM x1 + doxycycline 100mg BID x 10 days. Must distinguish from torsion.","Varicocele — observation","Hydrocele — surgical repair"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Epididymitis in sexually active men <35: STI-related (GC/chlamydia most common). Treatment: ceftriaxone + doxycycline. In older men (>35): E. coli/gram-negatives (urinary tract organisms) — treat with fluoroquinolone or TMP-SMX. **Board tip:** Distinguish from testicular torsion (surgical emergency): torsion = sudden severe pain, absent cremasteric reflex, abnormal testicular lie; Prehn's sign (pain relief with elevation) is POSITIVE in epididymitis, NEGATIVE in torsion. Any suspicion for torsion = immediate urologic consultation. Torsion window: 6 hours for testicular salvage."},
  {"topic":"Men's Health","course":"D120","q":"A 42-year-old man on testosterone replacement therapy (TRT) for 2 years presents for follow-up. His hematocrit is 55%. What is the appropriate response?","a":["This is expected on TRT — continue unchanged","Hematocrit ≥54% (52% if high cardiovascular risk): withhold or reduce TRT dose until hematocrit normalizes. TRT-induced erythrocytosis increases risk of thrombosis (DVT, PE, stroke). Consider phlebotomy.","Switch to a different testosterone formulation immediately","Start anticoagulation and continue TRT"],"correct":1,"rationale":"TRT monitoring: Check hematocrit at 3-6 months, then annually. Polycythemia (Hct ≥54%) is the most common serious adverse effect of TRT — increases blood viscosity and thromboembolism risk. Management: hold TRT until Hct normalizes, reduce dose, change formulation (IM injections cause higher peaks than topical), or therapeutic phlebotomy. **Board tip:** TRT monitoring checklist: testosterone level (mid-normal range goal), hematocrit, PSA (rise >1.4 ng/mL/year = urologic evaluation), liver function (oral androgens only), lipids, and mood assessment. TRT does NOT increase prostate cancer risk in men without existing cancer."},

  # ===== CANCER SCREENING (15) =====
  {"topic":"Cancer Screening","course":"D120","q":"A 30-year-old woman asks about Pap smear screening. What is the current USPSTF recommendation for cervical cancer screening?","a":["Annual Pap smear starting at age 18","Cytology alone (Pap) every 3 years for ages 21-29; HPV/cytology co-test every 5 years (or Pap alone every 3 years) for ages 30-65. Discontinue at age 65 if adequate prior negative screening.","Pap smear every 5 years starting at age 30 only","Annual Pap smear for all sexually active women"],"correct":1,"rationale":"USPSTF cervical cancer screening (2018): Ages 21-29: cytology alone every 3 years. Ages 30-65: cytology every 3 years OR hrHPV testing alone every 5 years OR co-testing (Pap + hrHPV) every 5 years — any of the three is acceptable. Discontinue at 65 with adequate prior screening (3 consecutive negative cytology OR 2 consecutive negative co-tests within last 10 years, most recent within 5 years). **Board tip:** Screen regardless of sexual activity history (HPV can persist for years). Do NOT screen women <21. Do NOT screen women who have had total hysterectomy for benign disease with no history of CIN2+."},
  {"topic":"Cancer Screening","course":"D120","q":"An HPV/cytology co-test returns: Pap = ASCUS (atypical squamous cells of undetermined significance), HPV = positive for high-risk HPV. What is the appropriate management?","a":["Repeat co-test in 5 years","Colposcopy — ASCUS with positive high-risk HPV is managed the same as LSIL (colposcopy indicated)","Repeat co-test in 1 year","Cryotherapy immediately"],"correct":1,"rationale":"ASCCP 2019 guidelines: ASCUS + positive hrHPV = colposcopy preferred (equivalent to LSIL). ASCUS + negative HPV = routine screening in 3 years (HPV-negative ASCUS has very low cancer risk). **Board tip:** Risk-based management: ASCUS alone (no reflex HPV) → reflex HPV testing. HPV-positive ASCUS = same risk as LSIL → colposcopy. HPV16/18 positive (regardless of cytology) → colposcopy. This ASCCP 2019 risk-based management replaced the older 'results-based' tables — know the basic pathways."},
  {"topic":"Cancer Screening","course":"D120","q":"What is the USPSTF recommendation for breast cancer screening with mammography in average-risk women?","a":["Annual mammogram starting at age 40 for all women","Biennial mammogram for ages 40-74 (USPSTF 2024 updated to start at age 40, down from 50) — individualize based on patient values and risk","Mammogram only if symptoms present","MRI instead of mammogram for all women"],"correct":1,"rationale":"USPSTF 2024 update: Biennial mammography recommended for women aged 40-74 (updated from previous recommendation to start at age 50). Ages 75+: insufficient evidence. Dense breast tissue: may benefit from supplemental screening (ultrasound or MRI) — patient notification of breast density is required in most states. **Board tip:** ACS recommends annual mammogram starting at 40 (or 45 based on patient choice). USPSTF uses biennial. Key distinction for boards: USPSTF Grade B (ages 40-74, biennial) vs ACS (annual at 40). High-risk women (BRCA1/2, family history): Annual MRI + mammogram starting at 30."},
  {"topic":"Cancer Screening","course":"D120","q":"USPSTF recommendations for colorectal cancer (CRC) screening — at what age should screening begin for average-risk adults?","a":["Age 50 for all adults","Age 45 for all average-risk adults (USPSTF 2021, lowered from 50) — multiple screening modalities available: colonoscopy every 10 years (gold standard), FIT annually, CT colonography every 5 years, stool DNA (Cologuard) every 1-3 years","Age 60 — most CRC occurs after 60","Age 40 — screen early to find all polyps"],"correct":1,"rationale":"USPSTF 2021: Start CRC screening at age 45 (Grade B) for average-risk adults, continue through age 75. Ages 76-85: individualize based on health status. Ages >85: recommend against screening. Colonoscopy every 10 years is the gold standard (therapeutic and diagnostic). **Board tip:** High-risk screening (earlier start): personal/family history of CRC or adenomas (start at 40 or 10 years before youngest affected relative), Lynch syndrome (start at 20-25), FAP (start at 10-12 years). IBD (UC, Crohn's): start 8-10 years after diagnosis, every 1-2 years."},
  {"topic":"Cancer Screening","course":"D120","q":"BRCA1/2 genetic testing: Which patients should be offered BRCA counseling and testing per USPSTF?","a":["All women over 50","Women with personal or family history suggesting hereditary breast/ovarian cancer syndrome (HBOC): first-degree relative with BRCA mutation, breast cancer diagnosed ≤50, bilateral breast cancer, male breast cancer, ovarian cancer, Ashkenazi Jewish heritage with breast/ovarian cancer history","Only women who have had breast cancer","No genetic testing — it causes anxiety"],"correct":1,"rationale":"USPSTF (2019) recommends: Refer women with family history suggesting hereditary breast/ovarian cancer for genetic counseling, and offer BRCA1/2 testing if appropriate. Risk assessment tools (Ontario Family History Assessment Tool, Family History Screen 7) help identify who needs referral. **Board tip:** BRCA1 mutation: 55-65% lifetime breast cancer risk, 39-46% ovarian cancer risk. BRCA2: 45-55% breast, 10-27% ovarian. Options for BRCA+ women: enhanced surveillance (annual MRI + mammogram), chemoprevention (tamoxifen, raloxifene), or risk-reducing surgery (mastectomy, salpingo-oophorectomy)."},
  {"topic":"Cancer Screening","course":"D120","q":"A 52-year-old woman asks about ovarian cancer screening. What is the USPSTF recommendation?","a":["Annual CA-125 and pelvic ultrasound for all women ≥40","USPSTF recommends AGAINST routine ovarian cancer screening in average-risk women (Grade D) — benefits do not outweigh harms (false positives leading to unnecessary surgery)","Annual transvaginal ultrasound for all postmenopausal women","Ovarian cancer screening is highly effective and should be universal"],"correct":1,"rationale":"USPSTF Grade D: Do NOT routinely screen average-risk women for ovarian cancer. Existing screening tools (CA-125, TVUS) have not been shown to reduce ovarian cancer mortality and cause significant harm (false positives → unnecessary oophorectomy → surgical complications, premature menopause). **Board tip:** CA-125: NOT a good screening test. It is better used for monitoring known ovarian cancer treatment response. Elevated in many benign conditions (endometriosis, fibroids, peritonitis, pregnancy). BRCA1/2 carriers may benefit from risk-reducing bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy after childbearing (not routine screening)."},
  {"topic":"Cancer Screening","course":"D120","q":"A 35-year-old woman has never had an abnormal Pap smear and had a negative co-test at age 33. She asks if she can skip her next Pap smear. What is the appropriate response?","a":["She can stop screening permanently if she has had two negative tests","With two consecutive negative co-tests, her next co-test is due at age 38 (5-year interval) — she does not need to be screened before then","She should have annual Pap smears regardless of prior results","She needs an HPV vaccine booster before her next Pap"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Co-testing (Pap + HPV) every 5 years for women 30-65 is an USPSTF Grade A recommendation. With a negative co-test at 33, the next screening is due at 38. Rescreening earlier provides no additional benefit and leads to unnecessary follow-up tests. **Board tip:** Extended intervals with co-testing are safe because: negative hrHPV dramatically lowers 5-year CIN3+ risk (<0.3%). Women who have not had HPV vaccination still benefit from vaccination up to age 26 (reducing future HPV exposure) but vaccination status does NOT change current screening intervals."},
  {"topic":"Cancer Screening","course":"D120","q":"Which modality is recommended for colorectal cancer screening for patients who DECLINE colonoscopy?","a":["No alternatives — colonoscopy is mandatory","Fecal immunochemical test (FIT) annually is an acceptable first-line screening option — positive FIT requires follow-up diagnostic colonoscopy","Barium enema only","Digital rectal exam annually is sufficient"],"correct":1,"rationale":"CRC screening alternatives to colonoscopy: FIT (fecal immunochemical test) annually — highly acceptable (non-invasive, home test), sensitive for CRC (74-79%), less sensitive for advanced adenomas. Positive FIT → diagnostic colonoscopy. Cologuard (stool DNA FIT) every 1-3 years — more sensitive but higher false positive rate. CT colonography every 5 years — non-invasive but radiation, bowel prep required. **Board tip:** Patient adherence matters most — the best test is the one the patient will actually do. Engage in shared decision-making about screening method. FIT is preferred for population-level adherence campaigns due to low burden."},
  {"topic":"Cancer Screening","course":"D120","q":"A 29-year-old sexually active woman with no prior Pap smears asks if she should have a Pap test today. What is the correct response?","a":["Yes — sexually active women should be screened annually","No Pap smear until age 21 regardless of sexual activity onset (USPSTF Grade D for women <21) — HPV infection in young women typically clears spontaneously and treating young women causes more harm than benefit","Only screen if symptomatic","Screen annually starting age 18"],"correct":0,"rationale":"Wait — this is a 29-year-old. She should have a Pap smear. Ages 21-29: cytology (Pap) alone every 3 years. She should be screened today. If her last screening was within 3 years and normal, she can defer. But if never screened, she should be screened now. **Board tip:** The critical board point is: Do NOT screen women UNDER 21, regardless of sexual activity. Starting at 21, cytology every 3 years. At 30-65: option to switch to co-testing every 5 years or continue cytology every 3 years. Women immunocompromised (HIV+): start at sexual debut, screen more frequently."},
  {"topic":"Cancer Screening","course":"D120","q":"A 60-year-old woman has no family history of cancer, has never smoked, and is a healthy BMI. Which cancer screenings are indicated at this visit?","a":["No cancer screening needed at age 60","Mammogram (biennial), Pap/HPV co-test (if not screened in 5 years), colorectal cancer screening (colonoscopy or FIT), and DEXA scan. Also: skin cancer awareness counseling, lung CT if 20 pack-year smoking history.","Annual CT of chest, abdomen, pelvis","PSA and CA-125 annually"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Standard cancer screenings at 60: Mammogram (biennial, USPSTF; or annual, ACS), cervical (co-test every 5 years or cytology every 3 years through age 65), CRC (continue through age 75), skin cancer awareness. No routine ovarian, thyroid, or pancreatic cancer screening. Lung CT: only if 20+ pack-year history. DEXA: all women ≥65 (or earlier if fracture risk). **Board tip:** USPSTF screening summary card: know Grade A and B recommendations. Grade A = recommend (breast cancer genetic risk referral, colorectal, cervical, HIV, lung CT in smokers). Grade D = recommend against (ovarian, prostate PSA ≥70, testicular)."},
  {"topic":"Cancer Screening","course":"D120","q":"A postmenopausal woman has abnormal uterine bleeding. What is the initial evaluation?","a":["Reassure — bleeding is common in menopause","Any postmenopausal bleeding is ABNORMAL until proven otherwise — evaluate with transvaginal ultrasound (endometrial stripe <4mm = low risk) AND endometrial biopsy (or office hysteroscopy with biopsy) to rule out endometrial hyperplasia/cancer","Repeat in 6 months","Prescribe estrogen — it is atrophic vaginitis"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Postmenopausal bleeding (PMB): most common cause is endometrial atrophy (benign), but endometrial cancer must be ruled out. Endometrial cancer is the most common gynecologic cancer in the U.S. Evaluation: TVS (endometrial thickness) AND tissue sampling (endometrial biopsy or hysteroscopy). **Board tip:** Endometrial cancer risk factors: obesity (excess estrogen from adipose), unopposed estrogen HT, polycystic ovary syndrome, diabetes, hypertension, nulliparity, tamoxifen use, Lynch syndrome. All postmenopausal bleeding → evaluate. Never attribute to atrophy without workup."},
  {"topic":"Cancer Screening","course":"D120","q":"Skin cancer is the most common cancer in the U.S. What USPSTF recommendation exists for skin cancer screening?","a":["Annual full-body skin examination for all adults","USPSTF has insufficient evidence for routine skin cancer screening in average-risk adults (I statement) — however, counsel all patients about sun protection, self-examination, and evaluate suspicious lesions promptly","Annual dermatology appointment is mandated","Routine screening is strongly recommended for all adults ≥40"],"correct":1,"rationale":"USPSTF: Insufficient evidence (I statement) to recommend for or against routine skin cancer screening in asymptomatic adults. However, PRIMARY PREVENTION counseling (sunscreen, protective clothing, avoiding tanning beds) in children, adolescents, and young adults (6 months to 24 years, and adults 24-64 with fair skin) is recommended. **Board tip:** Refer promptly for: changing mole (ABCDE: Asymmetry, Border irregularity, Color variation, Diameter >6mm, Evolution). Melanoma: surgical excision with appropriate margins. Basal cell and squamous cell carcinoma: very common, rarely metastatic, highly treatable with early detection."},
  {"topic":"Cancer Screening","course":"D120","q":"A 58-year-old woman with a BMI of 32 (obese) is concerned about her endometrial cancer risk. Obesity increases endometrial cancer risk through which mechanism?","a":["Direct DNA mutation from adipocytes","Peripheral aromatization of androgens to estrogen in adipose tissue causes unopposed estrogen stimulation of the endometrium, increasing hyperplasia and cancer risk","Obesity reduces progesterone production","Obesity causes HPV acquisition"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Endometrial cancer: Obesity is the #1 modifiable risk factor. Adipose tissue converts androgens to estrone (via aromatase enzyme) — this creates an 'estrogenic environment' causing endometrial stimulation without progesterone opposition (similar to unopposed estrogen HT). 10-unit increase in BMI increases EC risk by 30-40%. **Board tip:** Obese postmenopausal women are at significantly elevated risk for endometrial cancer. Weight loss reduces risk. No routine screening exists, but low threshold for biopsy in obese women with ANY abnormal bleeding. Progestin-only therapy (IUD or oral) can reduce risk in women at high risk who cannot have surgery."},
  {"topic":"Cancer Screening","course":"D120","q":"A Lynch syndrome (hereditary non-polyposis colorectal cancer — HNPCC) diagnosis is identified in a 32-year-old woman. What screening recommendations apply?","a":["Same as average-risk guidelines — start at 45","Colonoscopy every 1-2 years starting at age 20-25 (or 5 years earlier than youngest affected relative), AND annual endometrial biopsy/sampling starting at 30-35 years, AND consider aspirin chemoprevention","Colonoscopy every 10 years starting at 45","No screening — Lynch syndrome only affects men"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Lynch syndrome (MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2, EPCAM mutations): Colonoscopy every 1-2 years starting at 20-25 years. Endometrial/ovarian: annual endometrial sampling from 30-35; consider risk-reducing hysterectomy + bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy after childbearing. Gastric: upper endoscopy in high-risk ethnic groups. Urinary tract: annual urinalysis. **Board tip:** Lynch syndrome is the most common hereditary CRC syndrome (1/280 people carry Lynch gene variants). Aspirin 600mg/day for 2 years reduces Lynch syndrome CRC risk (CAPP2 trial). Refer all Lynch families for genetic counseling and cascade testing."},
  {"topic":"Cancer Screening","course":"D120","q":"A 48-year-old woman has a first-degree relative (sister) diagnosed with breast cancer at age 42. She is Ashkenazi Jewish. What screening modifications are appropriate?","a":["No modification — USPSTF average-risk recommendations apply","Annual mammogram starting at age 40 (or 10 years before sister's diagnosis = age 32, whichever is earlier), consider MRI supplemental screening, and refer for BRCA genetic counseling and testing given Ashkenazi Jewish heritage + family history","Mammogram every 5 years only","No screening until age 50"],"correct":1,"rationale":"High-risk breast cancer screening: First-degree relative with breast cancer + Ashkenazi Jewish heritage = significant increased risk requiring: BRCA genetic counseling (Ashkenazi Jewish women have ~2.5% BRCA1/2 carrier rate vs. 0.2% general population), annual mammogram + annual breast MRI starting at 30 (or earlier depending on risk assessment), clinical breast exam every 6-12 months. **Board tip:** Lifetime breast cancer risk >20%: annual MRI + mammogram recommended by ACS. Risk assessment tools: Tyrer-Cuzick (IBIS), Claus model, BRCAPro. BRCA1/2 mutation: 50-70% lifetime breast cancer risk, ~40% ovarian cancer risk."},

  # ===== DOMESTIC VIOLENCE / IPV (10) =====
  {"topic":"IPV","course":"D120","q":"USPSTF recommends screening for intimate partner violence (IPV) in which population?","a":["All adults regardless of gender","Women of reproductive age (adolescent girls and women) at primary care visits — Grade B recommendation; referral to counseling/supportive services for those screening positive","Only women who report abuse","Only pregnant women"],"correct":1,"rationale":"USPSTF Grade B: Screen women of reproductive age for IPV and refer those who screen positive to ongoing support services. Validated screening tools: HITS (Hurt, Insulted, Threatened, Screamed), HARK (Humiliation, Afraid, Rape, Kick), STaT. Universal screening in clinical settings increases disclosure. **Board tip:** IPV affects 1 in 4 women and 1 in 9 men in the U.S. Highest rates in women aged 18-34. During pregnancy, IPV increases miscarriage, premature labor, fetal injury risk. Always screen alone (not with partner present). Create a safe, private environment for disclosure."},
  {"topic":"IPV","course":"D120","q":"A 28-year-old woman with frequent ED visits for unexplained injuries screens positive for IPV using the HITS tool. She is not ready to leave her partner. What is the most therapeutic response?","a":["Tell her she must leave the relationship immediately or you cannot help her","Provide a safety plan, mandatory domestic violence hotline number (1-800-799-7233), validate her experience without judgment, document findings carefully, and offer to meet again soon — leaving is a process and safety planning reduces risk whether or not she leaves","Do not discuss it further since she is not ready to leave","Call 911 immediately without her consent"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Safety planning: Even if the patient is not ready to leave, safety planning reduces injury and death risk. Components: safe place to go, people to call, documents to have ready (ID, financial accounts), escape route plan, code word for help. **Board tip:** Stages of change apply to IPV victims — do not pressure immediate action. The most dangerous time is when a victim tries to leave — safety planning is critical. National DV Hotline: 1-800-799-SAFE (7233). Document objectively (quotes, body diagram of injuries, photos if consented). Mandatory reporting laws vary by state for adult victims (vs. child abuse — always mandatory)."},
  {"topic":"IPV","course":"D120","q":"Which documentation practice is MOST important when evaluating a patient suspected of being a victim of intimate partner violence?","a":["Note 'patient appears to be abused' as a subjective impression","Document objective observations: quote the patient's direct words, describe injuries with body diagram or photographs (with patient consent), record history in patient's own words, avoid editorializing. Create a medical record that can be used as legal evidence if needed.","Do not document to protect patient privacy","Only document if patient presses charges"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Documentation of IPV: Objective, detailed medical records protect the patient and can be used as legal evidence. Use direct quotes (e.g., 'Patient states: my boyfriend punched me in the face'). Body diagrams with injury location, size, and description. Photographs with consent. Avoid judgmental language. Document patient's refusal of services/referrals if applicable. **Board tip:** Medical documentation has been key evidence in IPV prosecutions. The medical record is the most objective contemporaneous account. SANE nurses (Sexual Assault Nurse Examiners) specialize in evidence-based documentation for assault victims."},
  {"topic":"IPV","course":"D120","q":"A pregnant patient screens positive for IPV. The perpetrator is her husband who accompanies her to all prenatal visits. What is the safest approach?","a":["Discuss IPV in front of the husband — couples counseling is the solution","Establish a private time with the patient alone at every prenatal visit (screen alone), use validated screening tool, provide safety resources, and coordinate with social work. Note that couples counseling is contraindicated in IPV — it can escalate violence.","IPV in pregnancy is not a clinical concern — focus on obstetric care only","Automatically call police without discussing with patient"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Prenatal IPV screening: All pregnant women should be screened for IPV alone, at multiple points during prenatal care. IPV in pregnancy: increases placental abruption, preterm birth, fetal injury, maternal mortality risk. Safety planning is essential — abusers often escalate violence during pregnancy. Couples counseling is CONTRAINDICATED in active IPV — perpetrators use session information against victims. **Board tip:** Routine screening question examples: 'Within the past year, have you been hit, kicked, punched, or otherwise hurt by someone?' Use AAS (Abuse Assessment Screen) for pregnant women. About 4-8% of pregnant women are abused — majority never disclose unless asked."},
  {"topic":"IPV","course":"D120","q":"A 35-year-old male patient presents with a history of emotional and psychological abuse by a female partner. What is the appropriate clinical response?","a":["Men cannot be victims of IPV — dismiss","Validate his experience, conduct a full safety assessment, provide information on resources (DV hotlines have services for male victims), and document appropriately. Male victims face additional barriers: stigma, disbelief by providers, and fewer gender-specific resources.","IPV services are only for women — refer him to men's support groups","Focus only on physical injuries, not emotional abuse"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Male IPV victims: ~1 in 9 men experience severe IPV. They face unique barriers: social stigma, shame, disbelief by others including healthcare providers, fear of being seen as weak, fewer shelters and resources. Psychological/emotional abuse is as harmful as physical abuse. The FNP must provide non-judgmental, equitable care to all IPV victims. **Board tip:** IPV is not gender-specific. LGBTQ+ individuals also experience high rates of IPV. The clinical approach is the same: screen privately, validate, safety plan, provide resources, document objectively."},
  {"topic":"IPV","course":"D120","q":"Which validated single-question screening tool can be used for IPV in a busy primary care setting?","a":["GAD-7 (for anxiety)","'Have you ever been hurt or threatened by your partner?' — a single-question screen with sensitivity comparable to multi-item tools; HITS (Hurt, Insulted, Threatened, Screamed) is a validated 4-question tool used in primary care","CAGE questionnaire","PHQ-9"],"correct":1,"rationale":"IPV screening tools for primary care: HITS (4 questions) — sensitive and specific for current IPV. HARK (Humiliation, Afraid, Rape, Kick) — 4 questions. Partner Violence Screen (PVS) — 3 questions. Even a single question ('Do you feel safe in your relationship?') can open the conversation, but validated multi-question tools have better sensitivity. **Board tip:** Screening must be done ALONE (partner not present), in primary language (use interpreter, not partner), in a safe private space. Screen at: routine well visits, prenatal care, mental health visits, ED/urgent care, substance abuse treatment settings."},
  {"topic":"IPV","course":"D120","q":"A patient with a history of IPV presents requesting documentation for a restraining order. What is the FNP's role?","a":["This is a legal matter — do not get involved","Provide objective medical documentation of injuries, care provided, and the patient's statements in the medical record. The provider can also complete forms required for protective orders and may testify as a fact witness. Refer to patient advocates or legal aid services.","Testify only if subpoenaed","Write a personal opinion letter supporting or opposing the restraining order"],"correct":1,"rationale":"FNP role in IPV legal matters: Accurate, objective medical documentation is the most valuable contribution. Can complete forms for protective/restraining orders based on medical findings. Can testify as a fact witness (what was observed and documented) — not as an expert witness with personal opinion. Maintain objectivity. **Board tip:** The FNP's role is NOT to determine guilt or innocence but to provide objective medical evidence. Refer to patient advocates, social workers, and legal aid services. Know your state's mandatory reporting laws regarding IPV — most states do NOT mandate reporting for adult IPV (unlike child/elder abuse which IS mandatory)."},
  {"topic":"IPV","course":"D120","q":"Elder abuse is a form of IPV/family violence. Which type of elder abuse is most commonly reported?","a":["Physical abuse","Financial exploitation (most common reported elder abuse) followed by emotional/psychological abuse — occurs when caregivers or family members exploit the elder's finances, property, or legal documents","Sexual abuse","Physical neglect"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Elder abuse forms: Physical, sexual, emotional/psychological, financial exploitation, neglect (most dangerous for health), and abandonment. Financial exploitation is the most commonly REPORTED form. Physical neglect (failure to provide adequate care) has the highest mortality. All healthcare providers are mandatory reporters for elder abuse in all states. **Board tip:** Risk factors for elder abuse: social isolation, cognitive impairment (dementia), financial dependency, caregiver stress/substance abuse. Use Elder Abuse Suspicion Index (EASI) for screening in primary care. Report to Adult Protective Services (APS). Elder abuse affects 10% of adults ≥65 annually."},
  {"topic":"IPV","course":"D120","q":"A 42-year-old woman with a history of IPV and childhood sexual abuse presents with chronic pelvic pain, fibromyalgia, and depression. What is the connection between trauma history and these symptoms?","a":["These are unrelated coincidences","Trauma (IPV, adverse childhood experiences) is strongly linked to chronic pain conditions (fibromyalgia, chronic pelvic pain), functional somatic disorders, depression, anxiety, and PTSD through neuroendocrine and neurobiological pathways (ACE effect, sensitized pain processing, HPA axis dysregulation)","Physical symptoms cannot have psychological causes","This represents malingering"],"correct":1,"rationale":"ACE (Adverse Childhood Experiences) and trauma: Strong dose-response relationship between ACEs and chronic physical health conditions. Trauma-sensitive care: acknowledge the mind-body connection, use trauma-informed approach (avoid retraumatizing), treat underlying trauma (EMDR, CBT, somatic therapies) alongside physical symptoms. **Board tip:** Trauma-Informed Care (TIC) principles: safety, trustworthiness, peer support, collaboration, empowerment, and cultural sensitivity. ACE screening (ACES questionnaire) in primary care identifies at-risk patients who benefit from trauma-informed approaches. Do NOT dismiss somatic complaints as 'just psychological.'"},
  {"topic":"IPV","course":"D120","q":"What legal requirement applies to healthcare providers who suspect or identify elder abuse or child abuse in all 50 U.S. states?","a":["Report only if there is certainty of abuse — otherwise do not report","Mandatory reporting: All U.S. states require healthcare providers to report suspected child abuse and elder abuse to appropriate authorities (Child Protective Services, Adult Protective Services). Reporting is required on SUSPICION, not certainty — good-faith reporters are protected from liability.","Report to law enforcement only, not social services","Only licensed social workers are required to report"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Mandatory reporting: Healthcare providers (including FNPs) are mandated reporters for CHILD abuse/neglect (all 50 states, since 1967) and ELDER abuse/neglect (all 50 states). Report to appropriate authority: CPS for children, APS for elders. Good-faith reports made without certainty are legally protected. Failure to report is both illegal and unethical. **Board tip:** Adult IPV (spouse abuse between competent adults): NOT universally mandated for reporting (most states leave decision to provider/victim except in specific circumstances like firearm-related injuries, which are reportable in many states). Child and elder abuse: ALWAYS report."},

  # ===== SUBSTANCE USE (6) =====
  {"topic":"Substance Use","course":"D120","q":"The CAGE questionnaire is used to screen for alcohol use disorder. What do the four letters stand for and what score suggests an alcohol problem?","a":["CAGE: Craving, Anxiety, Guilt, Excitability — any positive response needs counseling","CAGE: Cut down (felt the need?), Annoyed (by criticism of drinking?), Guilty (felt guilty about drinking?), Eye-opener (ever had a drink first thing in morning?). Score ≥2 = likely alcohol use disorder, warrants further assessment","CAGE: Coffee, Alcohol, Gambling, Exercise — lifestyle screening","CAGE is only validated for men"],"correct":1,"rationale":"CAGE questionnaire: 'Have you ever felt you should CUT down on your drinking? Have people ANNOYED you by criticizing your drinking? Have you ever felt bad or GUILTY about drinking? Have you ever had an EYE-OPENER drink first thing in the morning?' Score ≥2 has 74-91% sensitivity for AUD. **Board tip:** AUDIT (Alcohol Use Disorders Identification Test — 10 questions) is more sensitive and can be used in primary care. AUDIT-C (3-question version) is quick: 'How often do you have a drink? How many on a typical day? How often ≥6 drinks?' AUDIT-C ≥3 women / ≥4 men = positive screen."},
  {"topic":"Substance Use","course":"D120","q":"Medication-assisted treatment (MAT) for opioid use disorder includes which FDA-approved medications?","a":["Naloxone (Narcan) as daily oral therapy","Buprenorphine (Suboxone/Zubsolv — buprenorphine+naloxone), methadone (opioid treatment programs only), and extended-release naltrexone (Vivitrol) — all reduce mortality and cravings; buprenorphine is prescribable in office settings","Benzodiazepines as MAT for opioid withdrawal","Clonidine as the only approved MAT"],"correct":1,"rationale":"FDA-approved MAT for opioid use disorder: Buprenorphine/naloxone (Suboxone, Zubsolv) — partial mu-agonist, reduces cravings and withdrawal, prescribable by qualified providers in office; Methadone — full mu-agonist, only dispensed at licensed OTPs (opioid treatment programs); Extended-release naltrexone (Vivitrol) — mu-opioid antagonist, monthly injection, no abuse potential. **Board tip:** As of 2023, the DATA 2000 waiver (X-waiver) requirement for prescribing buprenorphine was eliminated — any DEA-licensed provider can now prescribe buprenorphine for OUD. MAT reduces opioid overdose mortality by >50%. Withholding MAT is an outdated practice; it is the standard of care."},
  {"topic":"Substance Use","course":"D120","q":"A 38-year-old patient presents after naloxone (Narcan) was used to reverse an opioid overdose. She is awake, defensive, and states she 'doesn't have a problem.' What is the most effective clinical approach?","a":["Discharge immediately — she is an adult and can make her own choices","Use motivational interviewing (MI) to explore ambivalence about substance use, provide brief intervention, offer information about OUD treatment options (MAT), prescribe naloxone for home use, and provide harm reduction resources — never lecture or shame","Tell her she will die if she does not stop using opioids right now","Refuse to prescribe naloxone — it enables continued drug use"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Brief Intervention in SUD: FRAMES model (Feedback, Responsibility, Advice, Menu of options, Empathy, Self-efficacy). Motivational Interviewing (MI) is the evidence-based approach — non-judgmental, collaborative, explores patient's own motivation to change. Always prescribe naloxone (Narcan) to patients with OUD or at overdose risk. **Board tip:** Overdose is a TEACHABLE MOMENT — the window for intervention may be brief. Naloxone prescribing to patients and family/bystanders ('overdose education and naloxone distribution' — OEND) significantly reduces overdose deaths. Good Samaritan laws protect those who call 911 during an overdose in most states."},
  {"topic":"Substance Use","course":"D120","q":"USPSTF recommends screening for unhealthy alcohol use in which patients?","a":["Only patients who appear intoxicated at office visits","All adults ≥18 years, including pregnant women — Grade B recommendation. Use AUDIT-C or single-question screening ('How many times in the past year have you had 5 or more drinks?'). Provide brief counseling intervention for risky use.","Only men — women rarely have alcohol use problems","Annual liver function testing is sufficient for alcohol screening"],"correct":1,"rationale":"USPSTF Grade B (2018): Screen for unhealthy alcohol use in primary care patients ≥18 years and provide persons engaged in risky drinking with brief behavioral counseling interventions. AUDIT-C is the most commonly used validated screen. Single question: 'How many times in the past year have you had 5 or more drinks in a day [men] / 4 or more [women]?' — any positive response warrants full AUDIT. **Board tip:** Unhealthy alcohol use includes both hazardous drinking (risky but not meeting AUD criteria) AND alcohol use disorder. Brief interventions (SBIRT — Screening, Brief Intervention, and Referral to Treatment) reduce heavy drinking by ~20% in primary care settings."},
  {"topic":"Substance Use","course":"D120","q":"A 45-year-old woman with alcohol use disorder wants to cut back and asks about medication options. Which medication has FDA approval for reducing alcohol consumption in non-abstinent patients?","a":["Disulfiram (Antabuse) — used for abstinence reinforcement, not reduction","Naltrexone (oral daily or monthly injection Vivitrol) reduces alcohol cravings and reward; acamprosate reduces withdrawal symptoms and supports abstinence. Both FDA-approved for AUD. Nalmefene is approved in EU for harm reduction approach.","Benzodiazepines for alcohol cravings","Topiramate (FDA-approved for seizures; off-label evidence for AUD)"],"correct":1,"rationale":"FDA-approved AUD medications: Naltrexone (oral 50mg/day or monthly 380mg IM) — blocks opioid receptors involved in alcohol reward, reduces craving; suitable for patients still drinking. Acamprosate (666mg TID) — reduces anxiety, insomnia, dysphoria of protracted withdrawal; better for already-abstinent patients. Disulfiram (250-500mg/day) — causes acetaldehyde reaction if alcohol consumed; requires motivation. **Board tip:** Gabapentin and topiramate have off-label evidence for AUD but are not FDA-approved. Naltrexone requires hepatic monitoring (LFTs). Must be opioid-free ≥7-10 days before starting naltrexone (precipitates withdrawal in opioid-dependent patients)."},
  {"topic":"Substance Use","course":"D120","q":"During a prenatal visit, a 24-year-old patient discloses she has been using heroin throughout her pregnancy. She is 16 weeks gestation. What is the most evidence-based management?","a":["Advise her to stop heroin immediately — 'cold turkey' is safest in pregnancy","Initiate medication-assisted treatment (buprenorphine or methadone) immediately — abrupt opioid withdrawal in pregnancy can cause fetal death. MAT is standard of care in pregnancy and does not harm the fetus. Neonatal abstinence syndrome (NAS) is treatable.","Refer to social services only, no medications","Advise Narcotics Anonymous only"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Opioid use in pregnancy: Abrupt withdrawal can cause fetal distress, miscarriage, or preterm labor. MAT with methadone (long-standing evidence, OTP) or buprenorphine (safer neonatal profile — less severe NAS) is standard of care. NAS in newborn (withdrawal symptoms) is treatable with morphine or methadone taper. MAT-treated mothers have better prenatal care engagement and outcomes. **Board tip:** Buprenorphine-exposed neonates typically have milder NAS and shorter hospital stays than methadone-exposed. Both are acceptable. NEVER advise cold turkey withdrawal in pregnancy. Social services, substance abuse counseling, and OB coordination are essential."},
]

